  <hr />

  <h2>Entry Strategy: Organic (No Partner)</h2>
  <h3>Recommended angle: CMMC for small defense contractors</h3>
  <p><strong>Why CMMC first:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Clearest regulatory pressure with enforcement timeline</li>
    <li>ICP matches perfectly: 10-200 employee companies with one IT person</li>
    <li>Pain is identical to NIS2: "the auditor asked and I had no answer"</li>
    <li>Nobody owns this niche yet</li>
    <li>Pricing advantage vs Freshservice/ServiceNow</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>What would be needed:</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>CMMC-specific landing page mapping features to CMMC Level 2 practices</li>
    <li>CMMC compliance report/export in the product</li>
    <li>Content: "How to meet CMMC asset management requirements without ServiceNow"</li>
    <li>Community: r/CMMC, defense contractor LinkedIn groups, CMMC events</li>
    <li>MSP partnerships — many MSPs serve defense contractors</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Timing:</strong> Phase 3+ (2027 earliest). Spain → Germany + Italy → France → then USA.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Accelerated Entry: DoD Channel Partner</h2>
  <p>A US-based investment fund or holding company that acquires, invests in, or distributes software to the DoD contractor supply chain. They bring the channel; DSM Control brings the product.</p>

  <h3>Why This Changes the Equation</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What the partner brings</th><th>What DSM Control brings</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Direct access to DoD contractors (the channel)</td><td>The product (nobody has this for the niche)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Credibility in the defense market</td><td>Unbeatable pricing vs ServiceNow/Freshservice</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deep CMMC requirements knowledge</td><td>Open source + SaaS flexibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Network of MSPs serving DoD</td><td>Rapid development (AI-managed model)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capital for US infrastructure</td><td>&gt;90% gross margins</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sales and onboarding in US timezone</td><td>Product roadmap and engineering</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Possible Deal Structures</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Model</th><th>Description</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Equity + distribution</strong></td><td>Fund takes minority stake, distributes to network</td><td>Aligned incentives, capital, skin in the game</td><td>Dilution, governance friction</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>White-label / OEM</strong></td><td>Partner rebrands for DoD market</td><td>Zero CAC, partner owns relationship</td><td>Lose brand, lose direct customer</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Revenue share</strong></td><td>Partner resells at markup</td><td>No dilution, keep brand</td><td>Less alignment</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Portfolio company</strong></td><td>Fund acquires majority</td><td>Maximum resources</td><td>Founder loses control</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Recommended:</strong> Equity investment + distribution agreement. Partner gets skin in the game; DSM Control gets channel access without losing control.</p>

  <h3>What Needs Validation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Product-CMMC fit</strong> — Does the current feature set map to CMMC Level 2 without major rework? Likely yes.</li>
    <li><strong>US data residency</strong> — DoD contractors almost certainly require US-hosted infrastructure.</li>
    <li><strong>Branding</strong> — White-label, co-branded, or DSM Control brand?</li>
    <li><strong>Exclusivity</strong> — Partner will want exclusivity for DoD. Acceptable if limited to defense + US only.</li>
    <li><strong>Dilution</strong> — Benchmark: 10-25% for strategic partner with distribution + capital.</li>
    <li><strong>Timeline</strong> — With partner, US runs in parallel with EU (2026-2027) instead of Phase 3+.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Impact on Roadmap</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Without partner</th><th>With partner</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>USA is Phase 3+ (2027+)</td><td>USA runs in parallel (2026-2027)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Must build awareness from zero</td><td>Partner provides instant distribution</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Organic CAC in a noisy market</td><td>Partner absorbs CAC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Need US-based support</td><td>Partner handles first-line</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Compete alone</td><td>Partner's credibility opens doors</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />

  <h2>Key Risks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Regulatory fragmentation</strong> — marketing less efficient than single-directive EU</li>
    <li><strong>MSP channel competition</strong> — ConnectWise/Kaseya/NinjaOne could add compliance features</li>
    <li><strong>Higher CAC</strong> — US SaaS market is noisier; organic may not suffice</li>
    <li><strong>Support expectations</strong> — US B2B buyers expect faster support (chat, phone)</li>
    <li><strong>Data residency</strong> — US gov contractors may require US-hosted infrastructure</li>
    <li><strong>Snipe-IT adds incidents</strong> — strong US community; if they add incidents, open source angle weakens</li>
    <li><strong>Partner dependency</strong> — if fund underperforms, US channel dries up</li>
    <li><strong>Distraction</strong> — running US + EU simultaneously could stretch thin</li>
  </ul>

